# {{ name | kebab_case }}

A Next.js (App Router) BFF generated by the `next` Moon template.

## ZITADEL Authentication

Authentication is built into this template and runs in the Next.js server
runtime. The home page is public; `/dashboard` requires a signed-in user. The
app uses OpenID Connect Authorization Code with PKCE. The browser receives only
an opaque, HTTP-only session handle; the user's minimal identity and OAuth
access/refresh tokens stay in Redis for at most eight hours. Logout removes the
Redis session and attempts to revoke its refresh token before redirecting
through ZITADEL's end-session endpoint. Server code can call the configured
Axum API with a bearer token using `fetchAuthenticatedApi` from
`src/server/api.server.ts`. Tokens are never returned to browser components.
The user's name and email come from the ID token when ZITADEL includes them,
and otherwise from the userinfo endpoint.
The flow follows ZITADEL's
[Authorization Code + PKCE guide](https://zitadel.com/docs/guides/integrate/login/oidc/login-users).

The login, callback, and logout endpoints are Route Handlers under
`src/app/auth`. The short-lived PKCE state between redirects lives in a sealed
cookie (`SESSION_SECRET`), and the session cookies are named after this app so
several apps can run on `localhost` side by side.

### Local setup

1. From the repository root, copy `.env.example` to `.env` and
   `apps/auth-server/.env.example` to `apps/auth-server/.env` if you have not
   done so already. Start ZITADEL and Redis with `moon run auth-server:dev`.
2. In the ZITADEL Console, create a Web application using Authorization Code
   with PKCE, and enable the **Refresh Token** grant in the app's settings.
   Request `offline_access` and the project audience scope. Register these exact
   URLs:

   - Redirect URI: `http://localhost:{{ port }}/auth/callback`
   - Post-logout redirect URI: `http://localhost:{{ port }}/auth/logout/callback`

   Enable Development Mode to allow these local HTTP URLs. Copy the
   application's client ID and the containing ZITADEL project ID. The PKCE
   flow does not use a client secret.

   To skip the Console, run `bun run zitadel:app -- --app {{ name | kebab_case }} --write-env`
   from the repository root; it creates the application with these settings and
   stores the client ID in this app's `.env` (see the repository README for the
   service-user token it needs).
3. Copy this app's `.env.example` to `.env`, set `ZITADEL_CLIENT_ID`, and
   generate a session secret of at least 32 characters:

   ```bash
   cp .env.example .env
   openssl rand -base64 32
   ```

   Set `SESSION_SECRET` in this app's `.env`. `APP_URL` and `PORT` already match;
   set the shared `ZITADEL_PROJECT_ID` in the repository root `.env`. Set
   `API_BASE_URL` to the generated service's local URL when one is available.
4. Run `moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:dev` and use **Log in** in the header.
   `/dashboard` makes a
   server-to-server `GET /api/me` call when `API_BASE_URL` is configured; it
   never forwards the browser's cookie to Axum.

To connect to an API, generate an Axum API with
`moon generate axum -- --name process --port 4000`, configure its ZITADEL API
application and key, then set `API_BASE_URL` to `http://localhost:4000`. Each
service needs its own ZITADEL API application and downloaded Private Key JWT
key file; services in the same ZITADEL project can share this app's audience
scope.

This template requires a server runtime; a static export cannot handle OIDC
callbacks or sessions.

### Production checklist

- Register a separate ZITADEL Web application for each environment: Authorization
  Code with PKCE (no client secret), the **Refresh Token** grant, and HTTPS
  redirect and post-logout URLs. Leave **Development Mode** off; it exists only
  to allow `http://localhost` URLs.
- Set `APP_URL` and `ZITADEL_ISSUER` (and `API_BASE_URL`) to HTTPS origins. The
  app refuses plain HTTP outside local development. Behind a proxy, forward the
  original `Host` and `Origin` headers: logout compares the request `Origin` to
  `APP_URL`.
- Point `REDIS_URL` at a Redis that requires authentication and TLS
  (`rediss://user:password@host:6379`). Session keys are namespaced per app.
- Generate a different `SESSION_SECRET` for each environment
  (`openssl rand -base64 32`) and keep it in your secret manager. It only seals
  the short-lived sign-in cookie, so rotating it interrupts in-flight sign-ins
  but not signed-in users.
- Signed-in sessions live in Redis for at most eight hours. They carry a schema
  version (`SESSION_VERSION` in `src/server/session.server.ts`); after a release
  changes the stored shape, bump it and users simply sign in again.
- Name and email are read from the ID token, or from the userinfo endpoint when
  the ID token omits them. Enabling **Include user's profile info in the ID
  Token** on the ZITADEL application skips that extra request but is optional.
- Failed sign-in, sign-out, token-refresh, and API steps are written to the
  server log as `[auth] ...` lines with the error name and code, never tokens or
  cookies. Send server logs somewhere you can search.
- The OIDC discovery document is cached for an hour. If refreshing it fails, the
  last good copy keeps being used, so a brief ZITADEL outage does not fail every
  sign-in.
- `next.config.ts` sends baseline security headers: `nosniff`, no framing, a
  strict referrer policy, a restrictive permissions policy, a limited
  Content-Security-Policy (`base-uri`, `object-src`, `frame-ancestors`), and HSTS
  in production. It deliberately has no `script-src` policy, which needs a
  per-request nonce; add one with a proxy when you need it.

Login always ships with generated projects, whether or not demo routes are
included.

## Running

`bun run dev` and `bun run start` go through `scripts/next.ts`, which reads
`PORT` from this app's `.env` (Next.js itself does not) and starts Next.js on
that port, on the Bun runtime (this workspace does not require Node.js). Moon
injects the same values, so `moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:dev` behaves
identically.

# Building For Production

To build this application for production:

```bash
moon run {{ name | kebab_case }}:build
```

Then serve it with `bun run start`.

## Styling

This project uses [Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/) for styling, wired
through PostCSS (`postcss.config.mjs`) and the shared design tokens in
`@vern/ui`.

### Removing Tailwind CSS

If you prefer not to use Tailwind CSS:

1. Remove the demo pages in `src/app/(site)/demo/`
2. Replace the Tailwind import in `src/app/globals.css` with your own styles
3. Delete `postcss.config.mjs`
4. Remove `@tailwindcss/postcss` and `tailwindcss` from `package.json`

## Linting & Formatting

This project uses [Biome](https://biomejs.dev/) for linting and formatting, and
`tsc` for type checking. The following scripts are available:


```bash
bun run lint
bun run format
bun run check
bun run test
```

`bun run check` generates Next.js route types, type-checks, and runs Biome.


## Shadcn

Shared components and design tokens are provided by `@vern/ui`. Import them from the package:

```tsx
import { Button } from "@vern/ui/components/button";
```

From this app's directory, the CLI routes shared primitives into
`packages/ui` through the workspace configuration:

```bash
cd apps/{{ name | kebab_case }}
bunx --bun shadcn@latest add card
```



## Routing

This project uses the Next.js [App Router](https://nextjs.org/docs/app) with
file-based routing. Routes are folders in `src/app`, and a `page.tsx` makes a
folder publicly routable.

The public pages live in the `src/app/(site)` route group so they share the
header and footer; `/dashboard` sits outside the group and renders its own
sidebar shell.

### Adding A Route

Create a folder in `src/app` with a `page.tsx` file:

```tsx
// src/app/(site)/pricing/page.tsx
export default function Pricing() {
  return <main className="page-wrap px-4 py-12">Pricing</main>
}
```

### Adding Links

Use the `Link` component from `next/link` for client-side navigation:

```tsx
import Link from "next/link";

<Link href="/about">About</Link>
```

The auth endpoints under `/auth` are Route Handlers, so link to them with a
plain `<a href="/auth/login">`.

### Using A Layout

The root layout is `src/app/layout.tsx`. Anything you add there appears on every
route. `src/app/(site)/layout.tsx` adds the header and footer to the public
pages only.

## Server Components

Components in `src/app` are Server Components by default. Add `"use client"` at
the top of a file that uses state, effects, or browser APIs. Server-only code in
`src/server` imports `server-only`, so importing it from a client component
fails the build instead of leaking tokens.

The header reads the session on the server, so pages in `src/app/(site)` render
per request rather than being prerendered. Reading the session never touches
cookies (Server Components cannot write them); only the `/auth` Route Handlers
create or clear the session cookie.

## Route Handlers

Create API endpoints with a `route.ts` file:

```tsx
// src/app/api/hello/route.ts
export function GET() {
  return Response.json({ message: "Hello, World!" });
}
```

## Data Fetching

Fetch data directly in async Server Components, or call
`getDashboardData` / `fetchAuthenticatedApi` from `src/server` for
authenticated API calls. For client-side data, this project includes
[TanStack Query](https://tanstack.com/query) through `src/components/Providers.tsx`.

```tsx
export default async function People() {
  const response = await fetch("https://swapi.dev/api/people");
  const data = await response.json();

  return (
    <ul>
      {data.results.map((person: { name: string }) => (
        <li key={person.name}>{person.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```


# Demo files

Files prefixed with `demo` and the `src/app/(site)/demo` routes can be safely deleted. They are there to provide a starting point for you to play around with the features you've installed.


